  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/6/64/Avengers_Vol_1_3.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Iron Man is chairing the Avengers monthly meeting in the home of Tony Stark, insisting that they <i>must</i> find the Hulk.&nbsp; He uses an image caster to send a ghostly image of himself out to the FF, Spider-Man and the X-Men, but none of them have seen the Hulk and seem too busy to help.&nbsp; Thor suggests that perhaps Rick Jones might be of help since he knows the Hulk personally.\n\nOut west, Rick receives their ham radio request and begins hunting through a desert (complete with cactus -- what is he <i>thinking</i>?).&nbsp; By sheer luck (<i>or plot laziness</i>), he encounters the Hulk, out near their old cave, and he convinces him to turn himself back to Bruce Banner using the cannon ray machine.&nbsp; But the dose apparently wasn't strong enough, and while sleeping, Banner again becomes the Hulk and smashes his way out.&nbsp; Rick runs to the Teen Brigade to call the Avengers to inform them he's on a rampage in New Mexico!\n\nTony Stark receives the message, becomes Iron Man, and summons the rest of the Avengers, each of whom finds their way across country to New Mexico.\n\nIron Man makes it to New Mexico first and is immediately attacked by the Hulk.&nbsp; Giant-Man, Wasp and Iron Man all try their hand at stopping him before Thor finally arrives and orders the Hulk to stand down \"or suffer my wrath!\"&nbsp; Hulk escapes by diving into an old mine shaft, later emerging next to a train, where he actually hurls its caboose at the pursuing Avengers!&nbsp; Hulk tosses flour into the smoke stack, providing a smoke screen that allows him to slip away unseen.&nbsp; The text reads \"throughout the countryside, the search goes on.\"\n\nHulk later sneaks aboard a truck and is dumped into a stream, where \"days later,\" he emerges in the \"cold Atlantic,\" exhausted.&nbsp; He's finally retrieved from the water by a ship where he spends some time recovering.\n\nIn the meantime, however, the reader is shown the Sub-Mariner monitoring the Hulk's every move.&nbsp; He meets the Hulk on a small deserted island, where the Hulk goes to \"plan my next moves against the human race that hates me!\"&nbsp; The Hulk mistakes Sub-Mariner for a \"puny human\" and attempts to hurl him away, but the Sub-Mariner can fight back, and once in the water, he proves he's a match for the Hulk, and the two eventually learn they share a \"burning hatred for the human race\" and should team up.&nbsp; \"Together, we can bring humanity to its knees!\"&nbsp; The Hulk consents.&nbsp; Naturally, they both think that once the Avengers are beaten, they'll destroy each other.&nbsp; Sub-Mariner has a \"command ship\" that he uses to take them to Gibraltar, where he contacts the Avengers to let them know that he and the Hulk challenge them to battle!\n\nSoon, the Avengers head to Gibraltar in Tony Stark's experimental deep-sea jet bathyscaph.&nbsp; Using some old abandoned WWII weapons, the Hulk and Sub-Mariner strike!&nbsp; Iron Man is hit by emery dust which stiffens his joints, and Giant-Man and the Wasp are forced to use a leftover supply of oxygen to blow the dust out so Iron Man can move again while Thor takes on the evil pair.&nbsp; They all eventually re-engage, and both the Hulk and Sub-Mariner attempt to take Thor's hammer out of his grasp, but there is no real chance of that.\n\nSuddenly, the Hulk abruptly changes back to \"Bob\" Banner and flees.&nbsp; \"Perhaps it's better this way!&nbsp; As Bob Banner, I've no hatred for the Avengers.\"&nbsp; As the Hulk is now absent from the fray, the Avengers finally begin to overwhelm the Sub-Mariner, who has now been out of the water long enough to weaken him.&nbsp; Thor leaves to go search for the Hulk, and Iron Man tries to pin Subby to the wall, but it cracks it behind him, letting water into the chamber, allowing Namor to be re-invigorated.\n\nNamor takes advantage of his opportunity to flee, and Thor says, \"I have too much respect for his valor!&nbsp; Namor has <i>earned</i> his escape!\"&nbsp; While it's a shame he isn't on their side, Iron Man declares they've made a bad mistake, and that, \"We may live to regret this!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "The action is lively, the guest villain is welcome, and the pages turn quickly.\nBut the Avengers mission statement of 'Fight the Hulk' is already wearing thin.",
  "subtitle": "Have Hulk, Will Plot",
  "review_text": "This third outing by the Avengers was largely filled with what both previous Avengers adventures have been, which is:\n\n<b><i>Need a plot assist?&nbsp; Gotcha covered!</i></b>\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=large; fonttype=bold; text= Just battle the Hulk!]\n\nThat is essentially all this book has been doing so far:\n[HANG] * In [LINK:1963:AVEN_1|AVEN #1|2026-11-13], Thor, Iron Man and Ant-Man/Wasp head out west to... <b><i>fight the Hulk</i></b>!\n[HANG] * In [LINK:1963:AVEN_2|AVEN #2|2027-01-08], the Space Phantom causes conflict within the ranks, resulting in... <b><i>fighting the Hulk</i></b>!\n[HANG] * Now, on page 2 of this issue, Iron Man says <b>\"We've got to find the Hulk!\"</b>\n\n[ASSET: left | BattlingTheHulk | width=21%] This leads to around <i>half</i> of the pages of the book devoted to... <i>you guessed it</i>... [SPAN: severity=info; text=battling the Hulk] (though admittedly a couple of them show Sub-Mariner doing it, as opposed to the Avengers).\n[BR]\n[BR] The Hulk is little more than a walking, talking <i>pressure valve</i>, designed solely to remove storytelling stress!&nbsp; If you don't believe me, then I suggest you go back and review pages 9-14, which was [SPAN: severity=warning; text=six straight pages] of... <i>well</i>... [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=do I really need to say it?]\n\nIf indeed Jack Kirby was partially responsible for the plot on this issue, readers can't deny that some [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=rinse and repeat] tendencies are beginning to take hold!\n\n[ASSET: left | SufferMyWrath | width=21%] [ASSET: right | HammerEnchantment | width=14%] I did at least get a kick out of Thor's confident speech to the Hulk on page 11, where he says, \"I order you to return with the Avengers, or <b>suffer my wrath</b>!\"&nbsp; <i>Heh</i>, while one has to applaud Thor for his supreme confidence, does he really think this will make a <i>difference</i>?\n[BR]\n[BR] Much later in the story, we finally get to see how the Hulk is unable to take the hammer from Thor's grasp.&nbsp; Its enchantment is <b>absolute</b>, and he simply can't budge it.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=Well done on that score!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nBut... despite the repetitive nature of the series thus far, there wasn't anything really <i>wrong</i> with the book.\n\nHaving the Hulk team up with the Sub-Mariner makes a good bit of sense, given their mutual distrust of humans, so that was a [SPAN: severity=success; text=positive element of this story] (and their brief spar on pages 16 and 17 was even one of the story's highlights).\n[ASSET: center | NamorAppears | width=67%]\n\nA pair like this seems <i>exactly</i> the sort of menace the Avengers were formed to confront -- and stop -- [SPAN: severity=success; text=so that was all good.]&nbsp; And I'm even okay with the Sub-Mariner's decision to go after the <i>Avengers</i> rather than the Fantastic Four, given that he was trying to accommodate his new partner, who probably couldn't care less about the FF.\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[SPAN: severity=warning; text=On the other hand...] there was also surprisingly little that felt truly <i>engaging</i> about the story either.\n\nThe Avengers battled the Hulk page after page, and yet the green-skinned behemoth was <i>mysteriously</i> able to navigate from New Mexico to the <i>Atlantic Ocean</i>, [SPAN: severity=error; text=completely by water!]&nbsp; This is one of the most puzzling aspects of the entire story.&nbsp; While page 15 <i>does</i> indicate that the search for the Hulk continued \"throughout the countryside,\" the text never gives the reader the impression it wasn't just the countryside of <i>New Mexico</i>, and it never even <i>hints</i> that the search continued all the way to the east coast!\n\nAnd yet, days later, the Hulk somehow ends up in the \"cold Atlantic.\"\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=error; text=(I'm no geography expert, but this borders on impossible.)]\n\nOn what map of the United States does one find a body of water that leads from New Mexico out to the Atlantic Ocean?&nbsp; The Gulf of America (known as the Gulf of <i>Mexico</i> back then) or Gulf of California, <i>perhaps</i> -- but the <i>Atlantic</i>?&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(I find this hard to believe.)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | UnholyTeamUp | width=15%] [ASSET: right | UneasyAlliance | width=10%] Once the Sub-Mariner enters the picture, he is again portrayed as having deep resentment of the human race, so their unholy team-up is at least <i>partially</i> justified, both of them having a beef with humanity.&nbsp; The editorial note is accurate in mentioning [LINK:1963:FF_Annual_1|FF Annual 1|2026-11-16] as his most recent appearance, one published a mere four months earlier.\n[BR]\n[BR] But the alliance is tentative, and both Hulk and Sub-Mariner secretly plan to do away with each other.&nbsp; Note the nice use of shadowing in the right panel over the image of the Hulk.&nbsp; This is one of the better visual elements that helps drive home the uneasiness of the team-up, [SPAN: severity=success; text=and I liked it a lot.]\n\nLater, Subby even states the obvious <i>out loud</i> when he tells the Hulk \"I have need of your brutish strength!\"&nbsp; This sends a clear enough signal to be detectable to even someone as dimwitted as the Hulk that he's being used!\n\n[ASSET: left | MutualLoathing | width=13%] The Sub-Mariner has never been shy about declaring his motivations aloud, and when the battle with the Avengers is finally over (after the Hulk had fled the scene), he makes it <i>quite clear</i> that he despises all humans, to which Iron Man points out that the feeling is <i>mutual</i>!\n[BR]\n[BR] Thor seems to believe that Namor had \"earned his escape!\"\n[BR] But Iron Man says they may live to regret it.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nAs with many of the Lee/Kirby battle issues, the action gets a bit tiresome and rarely is resolved when heroes battle heroes.&nbsp; The Hulk's transformation back to \"Bob\" Banner was [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=<i>completely</i> random] and seemed to have more to do with Kirby running out of pages than anything else, which soured the ending.\n\n[ASSET: left | BruceBanner | width=10%] [ASSET: right | BobBanner | width=18%] Finally, even though Banner was correctly identified as \"Dr. <i>Bruce</i> Banner\" earlier in the book, somewhere between pages 8 and 24 Bruce apparently transformed into both the Hulk... and \"Bob.\"&nbsp; (I'm no Bill Murray, but [SPAN: severity=info; text=What <b><i>About</i></b> Bob?])\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=error; text=How on Earth did such a blunder ever make it out of the editing phase?]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThis was a long one, at <b>25 pages</b> (as opposed to the normal 20), so I've given it a weight of 1.25 instead of just the normal 1.&nbsp; I have no explanation why the story needed so many extra pages, especially since Kirby probably could have trimmed a couple from the early battle sequence between the Hulk and the Avengers.\n\nThe issue was also <b>chock full</b> of inconsistencies, most of which I elected to detail in the Side Note below in order to stay focused up here on the main plot.&nbsp; They consist of <i>visual</i> anomalies (as in the case of the Wasp's wings or the Hulk's pants) and <i>logical</i> inconsistencies (such as Iron Man knowing where to find the X-Men's headquarters and the Hulk getting from New Mexico to the Atlantic Ocean entirely by water, not to mention the name of his alter ego).&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=Check those out below.]\n\nWhile it's good that the Avengers finally did decide to rein in the Hulk, to prevent him from \"running wild\" (as Iron Man states on page 2), one might imagine they probably should have thought of this <i>earlier</i>.&nbsp; Remember, as recently as the <i>previous issue</i>, the Hulk stormed out of their meeting into the streets of New York City!&nbsp; And what did the Avengers do?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=They went about their daily lives!]\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Does that sound like concern?&nbsp; Why did they wait until <i>now</i>?]\n\nBut though there was plenty to find fault with, the action kept the pages turning, and for an early issue of <i>Avengers</i>, that wasn't a bad thing at all.&nbsp; Sprinkle a bit of vengeful Sub-Mariner into the mix, and you have the makings of a compelling story, one that was perhaps [SPAN: severity=error; text=five pages too long.]\n\nThat being said, Lee and Kirby can't keep doing this forever.&nbsp; The repetitive -- and very prevalent -- \"battle the Hulk\" theme <i>has</i> to morph to something else, or the book will soon be unreadable.\n\n<i>Granted</i>, the title is still finding its identity, [SPAN: severity=success; text=so a little leeway is in order], but if this story proves anything, it seems the time has come to remove the Hulk's head from the upper-left corner box and [SPAN: severity=info; text=move on to something else.]",
  "side_note": [
    "[HANG0] * The Wasp is sometimes drawn <i>with</i> wings when she is depicted in her normal size (e.g. pages 2 and 5) and sometimes without (page 8)... or even <i>both ways on the same page</i> (see page 18).&nbsp; As her wings are really only supposed to show when shrunk to wasp-size, this is very inconsistent.\n\n[HANG0] * When Iron Man projects his image to the Baxter Building on page 2, artist Jack Kirby once <i>again</i> forgets to draw the building with five upper floors, showing only <i>four</i>.&nbsp; This is an ongoing problem I've pointed out many times already.\n\n[ASSET: right | SpideyCheckerboardMask | width=7%] [HANG0] * Jack Kirby's checkerboard rendition of Spider-Man's mask was a bit stark and looked markedly different than what he drew just the previous month in [LINK:1963:ASM_8_asm2|ASM #8asm|2027-02-14] (though he admittedly had Steve Ditko's help inking to correct any such obvious mistakes).\n\n[HANG0] * It was good to see the continuity of Iron Man reaching out to the X-Men for help, as invited to do so by the Angel on the final page of last month's [LINK:1963:TOS_49_iron0|TOS #49|2027-02-25], but it's just as important to note that Iron Man never actually stepped foot inside the Professor's School for Gifted Youngsters, [SPAN: severity=warning; text=so how did Iron Man know where to find the X-Men, as he is shown doing on this issue's page 4?]\n\n[HANG0] * It seems Rick Jones is back in New Mexico, after having been randomly placed in NYC in issue 2.&nbsp; I can't really get my head around this (and apparently neither can Lee/Kirby).&nbsp; Also interesting is that this issue's page 5 shows that both Thor and Giant-Man seem to know about the special relationship Rick Jones has with the Hulk.&nbsp; It's <i>true</i> that issue 2 does indeed show Rick Jones calling out for help as part of the Teen Brigade on its page 13, and that signal getting picked up by Giant-Man and the Wasp, but why would that imply that Rick Jones himself is able to \"control the Hulk?\"&nbsp; (We can only assume that the Hulk told the other Avengers this during his short stint as member.)\n\n[HANG0] * Throughout the issue, Jack draws the Hulk wearing shredded pants (<i>purple</i> of course), but both [LINK:1963:HULK_6|HULK #6|2026-07-07] and the two prior issues of the <i>Avengers</i> showed him wearing something resembling swimming trunks.&nbsp; As page 6 of this issue shows, Banner seems relieved to be back to his human self once more, we can assume that he has been the Hulk all during the time of the first two issues, so... [SPAN: severity=error; text=why the sudden change of clothes?]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(At least he has all five toes again instead of the 3 or 4 we saw last issue!)]\n\n[HANG0] * After Rick finds the Hulk while randomly searching <i>an entire desert</i>, the Hulk pulls what looks like an army Jeep out of a lake on page 5.&nbsp; Neither the lake nor the Jeep is ever mentioned again.&nbsp; How did a lake end up in the middle of a desert anyway?\n\n[HANG0] * Page 10 shows a shrunken Giant-Man and the Wasp taking cover inside of a tall ant hill.&nbsp; While there <i>are</i> ant hills in desert regions, they tend to be more flat and made of things other than the loose soil one might find in wetter regions.&nbsp; (Perhaps that aforementioned lake allowed the ants here to <i>find</i> such soil?&nbsp; Or was it the \"fast-rushing underground stream\" shown on that same page?&nbsp; Who knows!) [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(But where is all this water coming from out in the desert?)]\n\n[HANG0] * I find Jack Kirby's constant drawings of Iron Man flying with his [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=knees bent] one of the most <i>annoying</i> aspects of his artwork.&nbsp; Only he seems to believe that such a thing is possible.&nbsp; (Breaking [PAGE: scenario] wall here, but this <i>really </i>bugs me, and he does it <b><i>all the time</i></b>!)\n\n[HANG0] * The Hulk shows himself to be a real sourpuss on page 16 when he heads to the deserted island to (as he says) \"plan my next moves against the human race that hates me!\"&nbsp; As I already pointed out in my [LINK:1962:HULK_2|HULK #2|2026-03-30] review, this isn't even the first time the Hulk has considered violence -- perhaps even <b><i>genocide</i></b> -- against the human race.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(But for the Hulk, this seems to be nothing more than another day at the <i>office</i>!)]\n[BR]\n[BR] Comments such as these only prove that the Hulk is a menace, something the Avengers recognize, and seem quite <i>justified</i> in trying to stop!&nbsp; <i>Geez!</i>\n\n[HANG0] * Page 19 starts off showing the Avengers heading to Gibraltar on board Tony Stark's experimental deep-sea jet bathyscaph.&nbsp; The narration reads that \"<b>three</b> of the world's most colorful evil-smashers arrive...\"&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=What?]&nbsp; Only <i>three</i>?&nbsp; (I'm assuming Stan Lee omitted the Wasp in that count.&nbsp; What is <i>she</i> then?&nbsp; Chopped liver?&nbsp; Window dressing?&nbsp; Or is she simply not that colorful?)&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(shakes head)]\n\n[HANG0] * For some reason, Hulk's head is still in the upper-left corner box, even though he quit the team at the end of the previous issue.&nbsp; Hulk is obviously <i>in</i> the issue, but in this case, actively fighting <i>against</i> them.&nbsp; To contemporary readers, it could be interpreted as a sign that his exit isn't permanent.&nbsp; (<i>What other signal could it send, after all?</i>)"
  ],
  "decoder_ring": "About a year later, we find out there's more to this story, revealed by Thor in the pages of <i>Journey Into Mystery</i> 112 (JIM #112).",
  "highlight": "The Sub-Mariner joins the fun, forcing the Avengers to revisit their favorite pastime:&nbsp; fighting the Hulk."
}